  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Right knee pain with clicking after increasing workouts; difficulty running longer distances for marathon training.\n- Symptoms (onset/duration/severity/aggravating factors): Onset about 2 weeks ago, pain starts after ~20 minutes of running (around mile 2–3); pain worsens with flexion/extension exercises and long runs; occasional “giving way” sensation but not a full loss of stability; clicking with knee movements; transient swelling after the first run that resolved with ice; current knee not swollen and not painful at rest; able to fully straighten and flex the knee; pain mainly with activity (running/abs in gym).\n- Associated features: Mid-knee pain with motion, occasional tingling in right toes after runs; no persistent redness, warmth, or fever; no difficulty walking normally; no locking sensation currently; no significant fatigue or systemic symptoms.\n- Past medical history: Prior ice-hockey injury with a bruise several months ago; past acute UTI workup in ED (not directly relevant to current knee symptoms); generally good health otherwise; farsighted with prior LASIK; takes vitamin D; uses ibuprofen occasionally for joint discomfort; uses Aleve; uses melatonin; uses Xanax occasionally; smokes cannabis.\n- Medications/allergies: Ibuprofen; Aleve (NSAIDs); Xanax (PRN); Melatonin; Vitamin D; Cannabis; No known drug allergies reported.\n- Family/social history: Actively training for a marathon (distances increasing); high activity level; London resident; no smoking listed other than cannabis; no heavy alcohol use described.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: Not provided in transcript; plan discussed included physiotherapy assessment and possible imaging if needed.\n- Labs/imaging: None mentioned; referral to physiotherapist; discussion of possible knee imaging if swelling/heat/redness or locking develops; potential MRI if indicated after exam.\n- Key negatives: No current knee locking; full ROM (can straighten and fully flex); no significant left knee issues; no obvious systemic illness.\n- Red flags discussed: Possible concern for intra-articular pathology (cartilage/meniscal injury) or patellofemoral problems; patient worried about ligament injury (ACL) based on online info; doctor emphasized clinical assessment first and imaging if needed.",
